const faq = [
  { q: 'What does it mean if the raven is your spirit animal?', a: 'In modern spiritual and New Age frameworks, having the raven as a "spirit animal" is typically interpreted as: an affinity with transformation and change (the raven as shapeshifter); connection to hidden knowledge and the liminal space between worlds; intelligence and adaptability; and the ability to find light in darkness (echoing the mythological raven who stole or brought the sun). These interpretations draw loosely on Pacific Northwest Native American raven mythology filtered through the modern \'spirit animal\' framework — itself a popularised, decontextualised version of the more complex spiritual relationship between specific indigenous peoples and specific animals in their tradition.' },
  { q: 'Is the raven spirit animal a Native American concept?', a: 'The concept of the raven as a primary spiritual figure derives most directly from the traditions of the Pacific Northwest Coast peoples — Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian, Kwakwaka\'wakw, and others — where Raven is the creator-trickster who shaped the world. However, the generic "spirit animal" framework applied to it in modern Western spirituality is a significant simplification and conflation of diverse indigenous traditions. Many indigenous scholars and communities have raised concerns about the appropriation of "spirit animal" language by non-indigenous people, noting that the specific relationships between people, animals, and spirits in indigenous cosmology are complex, context-specific, and not well-represented by the popularised \'spirit animal\' concept.' },
  { q: 'What qualities does the raven represent spiritually?', a: 'Across the traditions where the raven figures most prominently as a spiritual entity, the consistently recurring qualities are: intelligence and cunning (the raven solves problems others cannot); transformation and shapeshifting (the raven can change form and identity); liminal presence (the raven moves between the world of the living and the world of the dead); prophecy and foreknowledge (the Norse raven brings news from across the world); and paradox (the raven is simultaneously creator and trickster, sacred and irreverent, bringer of light and associated with darkness). This paradoxical quality — that the raven defies simple moral categorisation — may be the deepest truth that raven symbolism captures.' },
];
